<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demeter</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demeter was the Greek goddess of grain, agriculture and the fertility of the earth, and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a>. The myth of Persephone's abduction by Hades and Demeter's search for her explained the cycle of the seasons and was central to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusinian-mysteries">Eleusinian Mysteries</a>, the most famous mystery cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Her sanctuaries were found throughout the Greek world, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> identified her with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceres">Ceres</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demeter.jpg" alt="Demeter" width="320" height="733" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Demeter_Altemps_Inv8546.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Greek cults of Apollo, Aphrodite and <b>Demeter</b> coexisted with local deities, and in the Roman period Panticapaeum and other towns had Jewish communities whose inscriptions record the freeing of slaves in the synagogue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the later fifth century Knossos struck silver coins showing a stylised labyrinth on one side and often the Minotaur or a head of Hera, <b>Demeter</b> or Apollo on the other, an explicit claim on the Minoan legends.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…father of gods and men, with his wife Hera; his brother Poseidon, god of the sea and earthquakes; <b>Demeter</b>, goddess of grain; Athena, goddess of wisdom, war and crafts; Apollo, god of prophecy, music and healing; his sister Artemis, the huntress; Ares, god of war; Aphrodite, goddess of…</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Iberian religion was polytheistic and shaped by Greek, Carthaginian and Phoenician practice, and Tanit, Baal, Melqart, Artemis, <b>Demeter</b> and Asclepius were known and worshipped in the region.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>This one began at the shrine of <b>Demeter</b> at Anthela near Thermopylae and extended its care to Delphi.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a></li><li><blockquote>Its sanctuary of <b>Demeter</b> Chthonia had a festival with a famous cattle sacrifice, and a nearby chasm was believed to be a short route to the underworld.</blockquote><a href="/state/hermione">Hermione</a></li></ul></section>
